#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""r66_issue_correction.py — post the final-mile correction comments to
#6 and #10 (the two R66 comments carrying statements the final-mile fix
supersedes) and verify each by read-back.  No issue is closed; no
duplicate issues."""
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, "/home/z/my-project/scripts")
from gh_api import req, REPO  # noqa: E402

PUSHED = "5105131 / a9568fe / 04c395a / 5b660e8 + the final-mile fix " \
         "b2feee85 (remote main, 19/19 blobs read back byte-identical)"

C6 = f"""**R66 final-mile correction (supersedes one sentence above).** Commits `{PUSHED}`.

The sentence *"the other three games' behavior unchanged (their packages untouched)"* needs an honest amendment: the games' behavior and adapters are unchanged, but **all four `.ps1game` archives were REGENERATED** this round. Reason: a GENERIC exporter defect (found by the continuation session's independent full-suite re-run — 4 failures the committed state had never seen) made `build_package` archive a path-string stub (`"/home/z/…"`, 50-65 B) instead of the file's bytes for every standard `(repo_path, arcpath, role)` entry — every r65/r66 package was structurally valid (manifest hashes matched the stubs) but content-hollow, and the earlier verifications checked structure + hashes only, never content.

Fix in the SHARED runtime (per the ownership rule — no game knowledge in the core, no core knowledge in a game): dict first element = inline JSON, str = source path; regression test `test_package_carries_real_content_not_path_stubs` (archive byte-equality + a stub detector). All four packages regenerated by the fixed exporter and their installed content READ BACK in fresh roots (`scripts/r66_content_verify.py`): flappybird `3b659f0c…`, tetrade `4362a5b8…`, buzzybee `3286aa17…`, metal_slug_x v0.2.0 `54fdacc1…` — same knowledge versions, real bytes this time. The r65 release assets are superseded by the r66 release (all four corrected packages + SHA256SUMS.txt, each download-verified in place). Isolation unchanged: the exporter is generic, zero game semantics moved into it; cross-game isolation tests green; the other three games' behavior verified unchanged by the release gate (flappy R61 records byte-exact). Full suite after the fix: **841 passed + 2 skipped + 1 xfailed; gate 15/15.** The issue STAYS OPEN."""

C10 = f"""**R66 final-mile correction (supersedes the package hash above).** Commits `{PUSHED}`.

The knowledge now ships in `metal_slug_x.ps1game` **v0.2.0 re-exported, sha256 `54fdacc10130833823e171ccf55e6467b3154a5bb602c93c61fac88b78fbff0c`** (the `cc5af6c6…` archive above was produced by the defective exporter and carried path-string stubs instead of content — the same generic defect hit all four packages; see the r66 release body for the disclosure). The re-exported capsule is also the SCHEMA-LEGAL one: the shipped capsule had briefly carried the non-schema status `TESTED` on event 56 (honestly relabeled `OBSERVED`, consistent with the other four R66 events) and had crossed the 64 KiB design cap (67,448 B → compacted to 65,302 B with every number/address/evidence pointer preserved; the dropped prose lives in git history, `reports/r66/` and the worklog). The capsule facts themselves are UNCHANGED: 56 events, the pay-frame law, the magnitude table +500 ×4 / +1000 ×7, `next_experiment` = R67. Installed content verified in a fresh root (`scripts/r66_content_verify.py`); suite 841 passed + 2 skipped + 1 xfailed; gate 15/15. The issue STAYS OPEN."""

for num, body in ((6, C6), (10, C10)):
    s, cm = req(f"/repos/{REPO}/issues/{num}/comments", method="POST",
                payload={"body": body})
    assert s == 201, (num, s)
    s2, got = req(f"/repos/{REPO}/issues/comments/{cm['id']}")
    assert s2 == 200 and got["body"] == body, num
    print(f"#{num}: correction posted + read back byte-identical "
          f"({len(body)} chars)")
print("issue corrections verified")
